## Background worker

The durable queue uses idempotent enqueue, bounded payloads, expiring leases,
heartbeat renewal, exponential retry, and a terminal dead state. Start an independent
worker process after building the service:

```bash
java -jar target/{{.ArtifactID}}-0.1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar \
  --spring.main.web-application-type=none --scaffold.worker.enabled=true
```

Set `WORKER_ID` to a stable unique process name when desired. Add Spring beans that
implement `JobWorker.Handler`; handlers must honor thread interruption and must not
log payload contents. The web process leaves the worker disabled by default.
{{end}}
{{if .Notifications}}

## Email notifications

Feature code calls `NotificationService.enqueueEmail` with a stable idempotency key;
there is deliberately no generic email HTTP endpoint. The worker requires the
following runtime-only configuration:

```bash
export SMTP_ADDRESS='smtp.example.com:587'
export SMTP_FROM='Example <app@example.com>'
export SMTP_TLS_MODE='starttls' # starttls or tls
export SMTP_USERNAME='mailer'  # optional only as a pair
export SMTP_PASSWORD='replace-me'
```

TLS 1.2 or newer, certificate hostname verification, and bounded connection/read/write
timeouts are mandatory. Queued email bodies remain sensitive database payloads until
your retention policy removes them; never log payloads or place SMTP credentials in
source, Blueprints, or AI context.
{{end}}
{{if .Files}}

## File assets

Set `FILE_STORAGE_ROOT` to a shared durable volume before starting the service.
Authenticated callers can upload, list, inspect, download, and delete assets through
`/api/v1/files`. Uploads are spooled by the servlet container and streamed into a
same-directory temporary object with a hard 10 MiB limit and SHA-256 digest. The
local adapter publishes with a create-only hard link, rejects unsafe storage keys and
symbolic-link directories, and compensates metadata/blob mutation failures.

Only public metadata crosses the HTTP boundary; the generated storage key is never
serialized. A production multi-instance deployment must mount the same durable volume
or replace `BlobStore` with an adapter that preserves the same create-only, bounded,
streaming, and compensation contract.
{{end}}
{{if .Tenancy}}

Authenticated users create and discover organizations through
`POST /api/v1/organizations` and `GET /api/v1/organizations`. Every business
request must send the selected organization in `X-Organization-ID`. Authorization
uses that user's membership role, and every business query and mutation includes
the same organization predicate; never bypass the generated tenant service or
repository methods.
{{end}}
{{if .TenancyMembers}}

Organization administrators can list members, create 72-hour email-bound
invitations, change member roles, and remove access. Invitation tokens are returned
only once; persistence stores only a SHA-256 digest. Invitation acceptance requires
an authenticated identity with the invited email. Keep member mutations inside the
generated repository transaction so concurrent changes cannot remove the final
administrator.
{{end}}
{{if .TenancyLifecycle}}

The organization creator is its initial owner. Active administrators may rename
the organization; only the owner may transfer ownership, deactivate, or reactivate
it. Transfer targets an existing member and promotes that identity to administrator.
Deactivation retains all tenant data but blocks new tenant-authorized requests.
Transfer ownership before changing or removing the owner; do not add a cascading
organization deletion shortcut.
{{end}}
